import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.RoundingMode;

// Written via pair programming, then sent for asynchronous code review.
public class DiscountCalculator {

    // Navigator suggested guarding nulls while the driver typed this method:
    public BigDecimal apply(BigDecimal price, BigDecimal percent) {
        if (price == null || percent == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("price and percent are required");
        }
        BigDecimal factor = BigDecimal.ONE.subtract(percent); // 0.10 -> keep 90%
        return price.multiply(factor).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var calc = new DiscountCalculator();
        // The reviewer later asked to add this edge case as a regression check:
        System.out.println(calc.apply(new BigDecimal("100.00"), new BigDecimal("0.10"))); // 90.00
    }
}
```
The pair caught the null guard live; the reviewer added the edge case afterward — the two practices reinforce each other.

